# ==============================================================================
# infra/bootstrap/backend.hcl.example          ✅ COMMITTED - template only
# ==============================================================================
# Copy this to `backend.hcl` (gitignored) and fill in the real bucket name,
# then initialise with:
#
#     terraform init -backend-config=backend.hcl
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS  -  partial backend configuration
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   A `backend` block CANNOT use variables. It is read before any expression is
#   evaluated, so every value must be a literal - which would put the state
#   bucket name, and therefore the AWS account id, into a tracked file in a
#   PUBLIC repository.
#
#   Terraform's answer is "partial configuration": omit an argument from the
#   backend block and supply it at `init` time instead. backend.tf keeps the
#   non-sensitive fields (key, region, encrypt, use_lockfile); this file carries
#   the one field we would rather not publish.
#
#   ⚠ To be precise about the risk: an AWS account id is NOT a secret. It
#   appears in every ARN, and you hand it to partners to configure cross-account
#   roles. AWS does not classify it as sensitive. But it is an identifier that
#   enables account enumeration and gives a social-engineering foothold against
#   support - so "do not broadcast" rather than "never expose". The repo is
#   public and the cost of omitting it is one CLI flag.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ⚠ THE COST OF THIS CHOICE, STATED HONESTLY
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   1. EVERY `terraform init` in this layer needs the flag. Forget it and
#      Terraform prompts for the bucket interactively - annoying, not dangerous.
#   2. A fresh clone has no backend.hcl. Copy this file and fill it in before
#      the first init.
#   3. CI must supply it too - either by writing the file from a repository
#      variable, or by passing `-backend-config="bucket=..."` directly.
#      (Step 7, deploy-aws.yml.)
# ==============================================================================

bucket = "tko-tfstate-<your-12-digit-account-id>"
